Part A: DNS Analysis Using CMD Commands
1. Press Windows + R.
2. Type cmd and press Enter.
3. Type ipconfig and press Enter to check the IP configuration.
4. Type ipconfig /all to view detailed network information and DNS server address.
5. Type nslookup google.com to find the IP address of Google.
6. Type nslookup youtube.com to find the IP address of YouTube.
7. Type ipconfig /displaydns to display the DNS cache.
8. Type ipconfig /flushdns to clear the DNS cache.
9. Observe the output of each command.

Part B: DNS Configuration Using Cisco Packet Tracer
1. Open Cisco Packet Tracer.
2. Drag one Server, one 2960 Switch and two PCs (PC0 and PC1) into the workspace.
3. Select Copper Straight-Through cable.
4. Connect PC0 to Switch, PC1 to Switch and Server to Switch.
5. Click Server → Desktop → IP Configuration.
6. Enter the following details:
   - IP Address: 192.168.1.1
   - Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
7. Click Server → Services → DNS.
8. Turn DNS Service ON.
9. Enter the following details:
   - Name: www.google.com
   - Address: 192.168.1.1
10. Click Add.
11. Click PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration.
12. Enter:
- IP Address: 192.168.1.2
- Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
- DNS Server: 192.168.1.1
13. Click PC1 → Desktop → IP Configuration.
14. Enter:
- IP Address: 192.168.1.3
- Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
- DNS Server: 192.168.1.1
15. Click PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt.
16. Type ping www.google.com and press Enter.
17. Observe the DNS name resolution and ping response.
Part C: DNS Packet Analysis in Simulation Mode
1. Click Simulation Mode at the bottom-right corner.
2. Click Edit Filters.
3. Select DNS and ICMP protocols.
4. Open PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt.
5. Type ping www.google.com.
6. Click Capture/Forward to observe packet movement.
7. Observe the DNS request travelling from PC0 to the DNS Server.
8. Observe the DNS response travelling from the DNS Server to PC0.
9. Click the DNS packet envelope in the Event List.
10. Open the PDU Information window.
11. Check the OSI Model and Inbound/Outbound PDU Details.
12. Observe the source IP, destination IP, DNS query, DNS response and port number 53.
13. Record the observations.
